-        "",
-        "Notes:",
-        "  - The remote cache is off for clients until SEMIO_REMOTE_CACHE_ENDPOINT names an http or https origin. SEMIO_REMOTE_CACHE_INSTANCE, _ACCESS (read-only|read-write), _UPLOAD, _COMPRESSION, _DOWNLOAD (toplevel|all), _TIMEOUT_SECONDS and _RETRIES override the defaults of the build policy; the access token is read from the variable named by SEMIO_REMOTE_CACHE_TOKEN_ENVIRONMENT (default SEMIO_REMOTE_CACHE_TOKEN) and is never written to disk.",
-        "  - Bazel renders the contract into its remote cache settings (endpoint, instance name, upload, compression, download mode, timeout, retries) and passes the token through a credential helper. Nx renders it into its self-hosted remote cache variables (server and access token); it cannot honour compression, download mode, timeouts or retries and leaves read-only to the server. status prints both renderings.",
-        "  - serve speaks Bazel's HTTP cache protocol (GET, HEAD and PUT on /ac/<sha256> and /cas/<sha256>) and the Nx self-hosted cache API (GET and PUT on /v1/cache/<hash>, 409 when an entry exists), each under an optional /<instance> prefix. A blob is refused with 400 unless its content has the digest in its address; an action result is refused with 400 unless it is a well-formed ActionResult whose output blobs are already stored (the policy can relax this to structure or off), and a result whose blobs left the store is answered as a miss. Bearer tokens come from SEMIO_REMOTE_CACHE_SERVER_READ_TOKEN and SEMIO_REMOTE_CACHE_SERVER_WRITE_TOKEN; without a token it refuses to listen beyond loopback; --read-only (or SEMIO_REMOTE_CACHE_SERVER_READ_ONLY=true) refuses every write; --tls-certificate and --tls-key (or SEMIO_REMOTE_CACHE_SERVER_TLS_CERTIFICATE and SEMIO_REMOTE_CACHE_SERVER_TLS_KEY) switch to HTTPS. One server owns a directory at a time.",
-        "  - The store is bounded and evicts the least recently used entries and entries idle for too long. It lives in the remote cache home of the cache ledger, so cache status reports it and cache gc collects it with the same bounds; gc here does the same from the command line, takes the collector lock, never blocks a running server and stops at the next checkpoint when the caller cancels. serve and gc publish progress while they scan the store; the output follows SEMIO_LANGUAGE or --lang and is printed in both languages when neither is set."
